// PLAYTEST: does the trained policy survive a party that actually fights
// back with everything it has?
//
// This is the scenario the whole result rests on, and the one the offline
// harness cannot answer. `arena::run_rep` plays the party's side as
// All-Attack — every slot, every round — so no companion Special has ever
// fired in a single one of the 5.7 million fights the policy was trained
// and measured over. Its doc comment explains why, and the reason is good:
// an arena that invented party decisions would drift from the game.
//
// The consequence is specific rather than general. The policy's whole edge
// is focus fire compounding — put a companion down early and the party's
// damage collapses for the rest of the fight — and **heals are the exact
// counter to that**. This party carries two:
//
//     Sentinel     sandbox (Def buff, L1) + redundancy_sync (Heal 10, L6)
//     Sprite       the soft link, and the target the policy will pick
//     Sub-Process  hot_patch (Heal 8, L1) + redundancy_sync (Heal 10, L7)
//
// So the policy was trained against a party that physically cannot undo the
// one thing it does. "Finish the wounded" is a weaker plan against someone
// who can un-wound them, and it can even be baited. The measured 0.324 ->
// 0.611 is therefore best read as an **upper bound**.
//
// A full party of three at level 12 is `BASE_PET_CAPACITY`, so this is the
// most the player can field without structure bonuses.
//
// The composition is chosen, not arbitrary. Measured at 40 reps on this
// seed, the policy's bite depends entirely on whether the party has a soft
// member to converge on — a uniformly bulky party is *immune* to it:
//
//     sentinel / sprite / sub_process     100% -> 12.5%   (this file)
//     sentinel / sprite / scrapper        100% -> 77.5%
//     sentinel / sub_process / rootkit    100% -> 97.5%
//     rootkit x3                          100% -> 100%
//     sentinel x3                         100% -> 100%
//
// A composition from the bottom of that table would make this scenario look
// reassuring while testing nothing. This one is at the top on purpose: it
// is where the policy is strongest, so it is where a heal has the most to
// prove. Auto-play also drops the fight from 31 rounds to 10, which is the
// snowball itself showing up in the clock.
//
// What to watch:
//   - Does a heal actually undo the focus fire, or does the convergence
//     out-pace it? All-Attack loses this 87.5% of the time. If two heals a
//     fight turn that around, the real difficulty gain is a fraction of the
//     headline and every number in the training report is overstated.
//   - Does the enemy keep piling onto a target that just got healed back
//     up? It re-scores every round, so it should switch — and if it does,
//     that is the policy looking smart for free.
//   - Is spending a round on `sandbox` or a stun worth more than attacking
//     now that the other side focuses?
//
// Run it through `./arena.sh policy-full-kit --ab` first for the All-Attack
// numbers, then play it — the gap between those two is the answer.
(
    player: Fresh(level: 12, zone: 3),
    party: [
        (species: "sentinel", level: 12),
        (species: "sprite", level: 12),
        (species: "sub_process", level: 12),
    ],
    opponents: [
        (species: "trojan", count: 4),
        (species: "trojan", count: 4),
    ],
    reps: 1,
    seed: 904,
)
